-- MAGIC %md
-- MAGIC # Ingesting Data

-- COMMAND ----------

-- MAGIC %md
-- MAGIC ## Prerequisites
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC In this notebook, we will explore and run an ETL process that will ingest a dataset, in accordance with conventions set out in the [TPC-DI](http://tpc.org/tpcdi/default5.asp) specification. In order to execute this demo, you must have run through the preparations outlined in the *01 Preparing TPC-DI* notebook.

-- COMMAND ----------

-- MAGIC %md
-- MAGIC ## Data ingestion
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC With raw input data ready for ingestion, we will explore and run a Databricks Lakeflow job that will create and load a collection of tables that implements the business rules and requirements as set out in the TPC specification.

-- COMMAND ----------

-- MAGIC %md
-- MAGIC ### Exploring the job
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC Open the job that was created in the *01 Preparing TPC-DI* notebook by doing one of the following:
-- MAGIC 1. Follow the link provided in the output of that notebook.
-- MAGIC 1. Click on the **Jobs & Pipelines** item in the left sidebar (open it in a new tab so you can continue to follow along with these instructions).
-- MAGIC 1. Select the job that appears in the Jobs & Pipelines tab.
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC Open the **Tasks** tab. There, you will see a DAG showing all the tasks for the job, feel free to click on individual tasks to explore the settings for the particular task.
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC 1. Let's select **ingest_ProspectIncremental**. This is a **Notebook** type task. Databricks supports various task types and this type simply executes a notebook.
-- MAGIC    * Draw your attention to the **Path** field, which specifies the location of the notebook to be executed. If we follow this, we will navigate to the notebook that was called by the task during execution.
-- MAGIC    * Let's examine the dependencies of the task by viewing the **Depends on** field. Here, we see that the task depends on two tasks: *run_custmermgmt_YES_NO (false)* and *ingest_customermgmt*. Referring to the **Run if dependencies** field, we see that the task will run if at least one of the dependency tasks succeeds.
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC 1. Now let's turn our attention to the task parameters. If we examine the notebook code, you'll notice there are no hard-coded names:
-- MAGIC    ```
-- MAGIC    INSERT INTO ${catalog}.${tgt_db}.${table}
-- MAGIC    SELECT * FROM ${catalog}.${wh_db}_${scale_factor}_stage.${view}
-- MAGIC    ```
-- MAGIC    This makes the notebooks much more portable, since there are no built-in assumptions about the catalog, schema, or table names. Databricks Lakeflow jobs make it easy for you to define the parameters at the task or job level and reference those parameters in the executed code using the pattern `${parameter_name}`. As you can see, the parameters for the current tasks are pushed down from the job parameter. During execution, the referenced parameter will be replaced with the values set in the job task.

-- COMMAND ----------

-- MAGIC %md
-- MAGIC ### Executing the job
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC Click the **Run now** button at the top right corner. This will run all the tasks in the job using the predetermined SQL warehouse. Once the entire job completes, you will be able to see the historical runs in the **Runs** tab, which lists all the previous executions, as well as the resulting status. This allows you to monitor and track the historical runs for performance improvements or error diagnostics. 
